## Introduction

The ordinal probit model of Albert and Chib (1993, s. 5): a latent
z_i = c + f(x_i) + e_i with unit variance, y_i = k when gamma_k < z_i
<= gamma_{k+1}, the first cutpoint 0 and the K - 2 interior cutpoints
sampled by a blocked Metropolis step on the log-gap transformation with
the latents integrated out (Cowles 1996; Albert and Chib 2001), then a
truncated-normal refresh of every latent, then the Gaussian sweep at
unit variance. Reach for it when the response is ordered categories: a
Gaussian fit to the codes treats them as equally spaced and gives no
probability.

Prerequisite: BART (Chipman, George and McCulloch 2010) and AddiVortes
(Stone and Gosling 2025).

## The model

The latent, the cutpoints, the log-gap prior N(0, cutpoint_sd^2) with
default 1, and the walk scale, from `docs/models.md`, "Ordinal". Link
to `crates/thiessen/src/models/ordinal.rs`.

## Example

```{r example}
source("_setup.R")
data <- ordinal_friedman(n = 400, p = 5, categories = 4, seed = 1)
control <- thiessen_control(tessellations = 50, general_params = schedule)
ordinal <- paired_fits(data, list(ordinal = control), seeds)$ordinal
summary(ordinal[[1]])
probs <- predict(ordinal[[1]], data$test$x, type = "probs")
head(probs, 3)
```

An ordered factor selects the family under the default control. Held
out: 500 rows with their true category probabilities. A Gaussian fit to
the category codes is the default comparator, through
`paired_fits(response = )`, which arrives with this article.
`MASS::polr(method = "probit")` is the parametric baseline; no
BART-family CRAN package ships an ordinal model.

## What it shows

Figure 1, mechanism: category-probability curves along a transect of
x1 at the other covariates' medians, truth, ordinal fit, polr.

```{r mechanism}
```

Figure 2, comparison: calibration of P(y = k), predicted against
empirical in ten bins, one line per category, beside the table (log
score from the category probabilities for ordinal and polr,
expected-category RMSE for all three, calibration error); second panel,
the posterior of the two free cutpoints, `cutpoint[k]` from
`posterior::as_draws_df()`, against their true values with the trace of
one.

```{r comparison}
```

Figure 3, boundary: the same with a linear latent surface, where polr
is correctly specified and matches or beats the ensemble; and K = 2,
where the ordinal chain reproduces the probit chain draw for draw.

```{r boundary}
```

## Validity

Stem `ordinal`, cutpoints covered. Mention the known-answer test
(cutpoint and cell-mean quadrature), the broken-sampler fixture
dropping the cutpoint prior, and the full-size cutpoint ESS check.

```{r battery, results = "asis"}
cat(readLines("figures/ordinal/battery.md"), sep = "\n")
```

![SBC rank-ECDF difference](figures/ordinal/sbc_ecdf_diff_ordinal.png)

![Geweke](figures/ordinal/geweke_ecdf_ordinal.png)

## Limits

State only what the module note supports. Candidates: the walk scale
2.38 / sqrt(n (K - 2)) is the Roberts, Gelman and Gilks rate applied by
analogy, with no argument that it is right for the log-gap
parametrisation against a tessellation ensemble; `cutpoint_sd` is a
prior on the log gaps, and its interaction with the fixed latent
variance and first cutpoint is not discussed; the offset c is the
probit rule at K = 2, extended without argument; the joint draw of the
cutpoints and latents as the first two blocks is asserted valid, and
the one-paragraph argument is not in the repository.

## References

Albert, J. H. and Chib, S. (1993). Bayesian analysis of binary and
polychotomous response data. *Journal of the American Statistical
Association* 88(422), 669-679. doi:10.1080/01621459.1993.10476321

Albert, J. and Chib, S. (2001). Sequential ordinal modeling with
applications to survival data. *Biometrics* 57(3), 829-836.
doi:10.1111/j.0006-341X.2001.00829.x

Chipman, H. A., George, E. I. and McCulloch, R. E. (2010). BART:
Bayesian additive regression trees. *The Annals of Applied Statistics*
4(1), 266-298. doi:10.1214/09-AOAS285

Cowles, M. K. (1996). Accelerating Monte Carlo Markov chain convergence
for cumulative-link generalized linear models. *Statistics and
Computing* 6(2), 101-111. doi:10.1007/BF00162520

Stone, A. and Gosling, J. P. (2025). AddiVortes: (Bayesian) additive
Voronoi tessellations. *Journal of Computational and Graphical
Statistics* 34(3), 859-871. doi:10.1080/10618600.2024.2414104
